data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The constraints highlight a key limitation: only four transactions can be open concurrently. However, a single open transaction can accommodate numerous read operations across a large key range while many other transactions begin and commit. This suggests a design focused on read-heavy operations within a transaction. The scale of transactions and the size limitations on the Java heap necessitate efficient memory management.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The constraint on concurrent transactions rules out full map copies on transaction begin. A persistent, balanced binary search tree (like a persistent Treap or AVL tree) seems necessary, using copy-on-write for efficient snapshots. The `BEGIN` operation should be constant time and space by snapshotting the root node. Open transaction limits ensure garbage collection can remove unreferenced old versions, minimizing memory overhead.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

