data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Rereading the constraints carefully, the timing of READ/RANGE records relative to other transaction commits is crucial. A transaction's read set and read ranges are fixed at its beginning and do not change with a ROLLBACK. The timing of READ/RANGE records relative to other transaction commits is not relevant for conflict detection. The key is whether a read range falls within another's commit.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Transaction conflict detection focuses on determining whether a transaction's read operations (reads and ranges) are affected by concurrent committed writes. Crucially, a transaction reads from its snapshot at `BEGIN`, regardless of other transactions' commits during its execution, which is confirmed by the provided evidence. A conflict occurs at `COMMIT` if a key read or within a range was written in a committed transaction that happened during the read.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Transaction conflict detection can be optimized to O(log W) or O(1) per operation by using commit sequence numbers and key-specific mappings. A global `lastWriteCommitSeq` map tracks the latest commit sequence for each key, and a sorted structure manages all written keys. The goal is efficient conflict detection based on commit sequence number comparison. Still need to verify.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Transaction conflict detection focuses on individual keys, easily extended to ranges using an AVL tree. The `lastCommitSeq` approach efficiently identifies conflicts based on commit sequence number comparisons. For a range query, we check if the `lastCommitSeq` of any key within the range exceeds the transaction's begin sequence. This quickly determines if any committed transaction modified keys within the range after the transaction's start.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

