data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checking `RANGE` records:\n- Range query over AVL: $O(\\log N + M)$, where $N$ is the number of all nodes, and $M$ is the number of keys within the range. The AVL maintains a tree of *all* written keys.\n- Checking $R_2$ `RANGE` records takes $O(R_2 * (log N + M))$ time. Given the 16 MiB size limit, the memory impact remains manageable.\n\nThe total conflict-detection complexity at transaction commit is O($R_1 + R_2 * (log N + M)$), which reduces the number of operations.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checking each `RANGE` query within the committed keys tree takes O(log M) time, where M represents the number of distinct written keys. Range queries in a binary search tree use path splitting to determine the range.  Subtrees can be pruned if their `maxSubtreeCommitSeq` is less than or equal to a bound, or if any key in the range has a `lastCommitSeq` greater than the transaction's begin sequence. This approach provides O(1) time if nothing has been committed and reduces the time of operations.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Conflict detection complexity analysis reveals a manageable O(log M) time per range check, where M is the number of distinct written keys, and the whole range is done by tree traversal.  Optimized with `lastCommitSeq` to avoid unnecessary node visits, reducing the total operations. The total number of nodes visited is at most 15M in the worst case. Further, each `RANGE` is evaluated at most once at commit.\n\nA careful trace of the code shows `DELETE` operations where the key was not present, which adheres to Section 4 and R22 in the documentation.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The analysis focuses on the handling of `DELETE` operations and their impact on conflict detection, adhering to documentation sections R22 and R23b. Specifically, `DELETE` operations, even when a key is absent, constitute writes that can trigger conflicts. The write list stores these operations, and the `lastCommitMap` and `writtenKeysTree` are updated *only* upon transaction commit. This confirms that these absent key deletions are indeed included.\n\nAddressing the case of an invalid range query `RANGE(lo, hi)` where `lo > hi`, the logic correctly identifies no key $K$ satisfying the conditions, thus avoiding any conflict for such a range.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

