data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `ISO_8859_1` encoding and its interaction with Java string creation are crucial for memory efficiency. Since all characters are within the Latin-1 range, direct byte-to-char mapping is valid. This allows for optimized `String` creation, potentially avoiding unnecessary allocations. Analyzing the memory impact of transactions with numerous read operations requires further investigation.\n\nCalculating the memory usage for `T.readSet` (a `HashSet<String>`) and `T.readRanges` is essential. With up to 500,000 read operations per transaction, the memory footprint needs careful evaluation. Specifically, two flat `String[]` for low and high bounds, instead of wrapper objects, could reduce memory usage. Once transactions commit or abort, garbage collection will free these resources.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"A `HashSet<String>` for `readSet` and flat `String[]` arrays for low and high bounds of `readRanges` appear sufficient for memory efficiency, even with up to 500,000 read operations. Transient `String` objects are created during log processing, minimizing memory overhead if duplicate reads occur. Even with numerous, distinct range queries, memory remains within bounds.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R1 and R2 are confirmed: handling null and empty logs correctly. Empty result construction matches specifications. Now, analyzing R3 and R4.\n\n-   **R3**: If `L < 8` (header size), throw `new WalException(\"truncated log: length < 8\")`.\n-   **R4**: Read header (8 bytes): `magic` (4 bytes, expected 0x9f29d290), `version` (4 bytes, expected 1). If either mismatch, throw `WalException`.\n\nVerifying R3 and R4 requires reading the header and confirming magic/version. Throw `WalException` on length or value mismatch. The code should now handle these checks.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

