data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checksum verification is mathematically sound: the cumulative sums `s1` and `s2` are congruent modulo 255 to their reduced counterparts throughout the processing loop. This guarantees that checksum validation correctly detects any data corruption within a log record. The modulo operation never generates an incorrect sum.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Checksum calculation is confirmed, ensuring no overflow issues due to carefully analyzed bounds. The modular arithmetic maintains data integrity during processing. Thoroughly tested the edge cases and sample values, confirming the correctness of the checksum algorithm. This finalizes record validation.\n\nThe primary focus is now on efficiently collecting transaction IDs during Phase 2. The memory constraint of 16 MiB requires memory-efficient data structures. Optimizing the process, using the `long[]` structure, prevents unnecessary object creation. Further analysis is now directed toward replaying the log efficiently within the given constraints, aiming to minimize the total memory footprint.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Fletcher-16 checksum computation is significantly optimized by delaying modulo operations until the end of the record, verified against the specification and test cases. This approach maintains correctness and drastically improves performance. The stored checksum byte order is verified as well.\n\nThe checksum validation checks are correctly implemented according to specifications and test cases, adhering to requirements R10, R11, and R12. The code now accurately identifies and handles both intact records and truncated tails. The current implementation now thoroughly and correctly validates all records encountered.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

