data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R13 validation confirms: BEGIN, COMMIT, and ABORT records require a payload length of 4. PUT records must have a payload length of at least 5, plus the key length and value length. DELETE records also require a minimum payload length to check all the data length parameters.\n\nThe check for `RANGE` records (R13) is performed as follows: If the log size is less than 9, or if the `high` bound is less than the `low` bound, the code throws `MALFORMED_PAYLOAD` at `p`.\n\nThe `RANGE` operation (R13) is checked as follows: The lower bound length `lo` and upper bound length `hi` are verified. A minimum payload of 9 is enforced for valid ranges. Invalidate, if upper bound comes before the low bound, the code throws `MALFORMED_PAYLOAD` exception.\n\nBEGIN, COMMIT, and ABORT types are checked for record malformations (R14-R16), and duplicate transaction IDs are now handled with a hash set and the sentinel value. The code is now working correctly. The logic confirms proper transaction ID assignment and management.\n\nThe log replay must correctly apply operations to the key-value store, handling transaction conflicts and data consistency. Analyzing the structure of the data:\n-   `BEGIN` records mark transaction start.\n-   `COMMIT`/`ABORT` records end a transaction.\n-   `PUT` creates or updates a key-value pair.\n-   `DELETE` removes a key.\n-   `RANGE` performs range queries.\n\nThe conflict detection must ensure that each committed transaction operates within the read and write isolation specified. The current approach focuses on using commit sequence numbers to track changes, and efficiently identify conflicts during COMMIT/ABORT.\n\nThe commit process manages conflict detection by tracking transaction sequence numbers and modifying keys in the transaction write list. The `lastCommitS