data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`countLt` and `countLeq` functions are crucial for range queries, using iterative while loops to avoid recursion and allocations, guaranteeing logarithmic time complexity. Tracing with examples confirms their accurate computation of keys within a range. The analysis demonstrates the efficiency of these functions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial suspicion regarding the `lastCommitMap`’s redundancy with `WrittenNode` is correct; `WrittenNode` already stores the necessary `key -> sequence number` mapping. Comparing lookup efficiency, a `StringIntMap` (using primitive arrays) offers O(1) reads, significantly faster and with less memory overhead than `WrittenNode`'s O(log M) lookup. Using a primitive map avoids overhead from wrapper objects and tree traversal. This optimization enables faster and more memory-efficient conflict detection.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

