data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The initial concern about potential hash collisions with `String.hashCode()` warrants immediate investigation, particularly with keys up to 32 bytes in length. The hypothesis is an adversary could craft inputs to trigger a `HashMap` or `StringIntMap` performance degradation. However, it's critical to note Java's `HashMap` automatically converts buckets to Red-Black Trees when they reach `TREEIFY_THRESHOLD`.\n\nThis auto-treeification feature, where string keys implement `Comparable<String>`, guarantees O(log N) worst-case lookup time even with hash collisions. This mitigation eliminates the risk of O(N^2) behavior due to hash collisions, ensuring the chosen data structures maintain their expected performance characteristics, even under adversarial conditions. The design relies on the O(log N) worst-case scenario.\n\nThe Java HashMap is robust to collisions as the buckets auto-convert the bucket into Red-Black Tree. This ensures O(log N) lookup.\n\nThe approach for handling range queries should be reevaluated because the string keys are unique.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The initial plan to use a custom hash map was flawed; vulnerability to hash collision attacks poses a risk, with potential O(n^2) behavior. Java's `HashMap` treeifies colliding `String` keys, automatically mitigating the risk, and ensuring O(log n) performance. This realization shifts the focus back to leveraging standard data structures for conflict detection, given their inherent resilience. The approach of using an optimized AVL tree now appears to be an ideal solution for conflict detection. The AVL tree is a balanced tree.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `HashMap` for the final state map is now created and populated using an in-order AVL tree traversal to ensure O(S) time complexity, where S represents the number of key-value pairs in the state. No recursive stack overflow is possible, because the call stack is approximately $1.44 log_2(200,002)$. This method is both efficient and memory-safe.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

