data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The initial approach involved an AVL tree and sequence-number-based conflict detection, aiming for efficient read and write operations within transaction constraints. It appeared suitable, given the need for persistent data structures and constant-time `BEGIN` operations. Memory usage scales linearly with writes, suggesting optimizations via in-place mutation and path-copying.\n\nConflict detection uses commit sequence numbers with key-specific mappings. A `lastWriteCommitSeq` map tracks the latest commit for each key. For range queries, subtree pruning within the AVL tree, based on sequence numbers, maintains efficiency. Initial range query complexity analysis concluded efficient O(log S + W) retrieval times even for large datasets.\n\nFocus shifts to identifying invalid range queries where lo > hi, correctly handled by avoiding conflicts. The read set and read ranges are append-only. No operations remove entries, and `ROLLBACK` does not affect the read set or read ranges. A transaction's own writes are not considered in the conflict detection.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

