data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Phase 1, verifying record length and checksums, ensures all byte accesses remain within `log` bounds. Specifically, validations R7-R10 and length checks guarantee `p + 5 + n <= L`, making all index accesses safe during record parsing. Similarly, Phase 2, starting at `p`, utilizes Phase 1's validation to ensure the entire payload byte accesses in `R13..R16a` are within bounds.\n\nKey aspects in the validation:\n-   Each byte access in the logs is validated against array bounds in `log`\n-   All checksum calculations (Fletcher-16) and key/value validations are implemented correctly\n-   R13..R16 ensure correct payload and transaction ID management\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R3 and R4 were thoroughly validated to prevent out-of-bounds array access. Validations R7-R10 and length checks guarantee `p + 5 + n <= L`, and access checks ensure the safety of byte indices during the parsing process. In Phase 2, starting at `p`, the logic guarantees all the indices are within bounds. The structure of each record type ensures array indices never go out-of-bounds in all cases.\n\nSpecific Examples:\n*   `PUT` operations are checked `5 + k + v == n`, the code verifies all index accesses as safe.\n*   `DELETE` operations check `5 + k == n`, the code verifies all index accesses are safe.\n*   `SAVEPOINT`, `ROLLBACK` has `n == 5`, therefore all access indices are validated.\n*   `CHECKPOINT` checks `n == 1 + 4 * c`, all the indices validated are safe, based on the record type.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Rereading and re-evaluating byte access guarantees safety across log operations, focusing on the implications of each record type and related operations. These checks involve verifying bounds like `p + 5 + n <= L` to validate array indices, crucial to maintaining safety. The validation logic ensures data integrity during record parsing.\n\nSpecific examples are evaluated for `PUT`, `DELETE`, `SAVEPOINT`, and `CHECKPOINT` operations to confirm index bounds. The `RANGE` record verification involves checks of `lo` and `hi` bounds, and the expected count ensuring all indices remain valid for all operations. This confirms complete bounds checking.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-689b7469-a194-4d8e-ab6b-cfd8bb69e42a","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795554462,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The unsigned 32-bit transaction ID processing confirms correct handling of the full range (1 to 4294967295), including maximum values, ensuring correct comparisons and no overflow errors. The integer hash set correctly stores and compares the `long` transaction IDs, ensuring their order during retrieval of incomplete transactions for R26. The primitive int[] array can handle the large 32-bit transaction ID range, preserving numeric order for comparison and sorting operations, and avoiding potential issues.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

